#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092063 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 5e^{u} \\\\\n&= 5e^{5x + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{u}\\ln (2) \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 2^{u}\\ln (2) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 2^{1 - x}\\ln (2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2}$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\cdot (-2x) \\\\\n&=-2xe^{u} \\\\\n&=-2xe^{-x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
